  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern early this afternoon is defined\nby a weak shortwave over the far southern AZ/NM border extending\ninto NE Sonora Mexico, a trough of low pressure over the \nPacific Northwest/northern Great Basin and a strong subtropical \nridge spread out over the entire southeast U.S.. The latest GOES\nprecipitable water imagery indicated a much drier airmass across\nSoutheast Arizona than what has been residing over us for the\nfirst half of the month (PWATs between 0.80 and 1.00 inch with \npockets of 0.65 to 0.80 in far WRN Pima and SW Cochise). Still\nenough moisture hanging out along the AZ/NM border today, and so\nwith the weak disturbance around, there will remain a slight\nchance of showers/thunderstorms into the early evening hours. \n\nThe dry air will continue to advect into our neck of the woods \nSaturday, with PWATs between 0.50 and 0.65 inches anticipated \nacross the entire CWA. This will obviously shut us down completely\nfor storms this weekend.\n\nTypically, this enthusiastic drying usually throws an exclamation\npoint on a waning Monsoon season, but this year it appears the \ntransition into our tropical-season/October may be murky. With\nthe northern stream becoming more active and amplified, the\ntrough across the Pacific Northwest/Great Basin and follow-up\nkicker low currently near 140W both track east this weekend, \nshoving the subtropical ridge SW into northern Mexico. The \nresulting southerly flow across Sonora/Chihuahua MX will draw \nmoisture northward again into the eastern portions of Southeast \nArizona, mainly east of Tucson. Depending on the location of the\nsubtropical high and the kicker low that sets-up across the \nGreat Basin, we should have enough lift/moisture around Tuesday \nfor scattered showers and thunderstorms east of Tucson with \nmoisture available for recycling the second half of next week.\n\nThe extended is less clear. The NHC has identified Invest 99E \nas a broad area of low pressure 800 miles SSW of SW Mexico that \nhas an 80 percent chance of forming into a tropical system \nthrough 7 days. There are differences within the ensemble models\non how they want to track this feature. The 18/12Z ECMWF and AI\nensembles are the most aggressive pulling this feature north \nalong the coast of Mexico to east of the tip of the Baja whereas\nthe 18/12Z GEFS ensembles pushes it well to the west of the \nBaja tip. This is mainly due to how the models shunt that \nsubtropical ridge into NRN Mexico. The ECMWF and AI models \nweaken the feature significantly whereas the GEFS keeps it as a \ndominant feature preventing it from moving farther north. My \nhunch tells me that the GEFS are more correct with their \nsolution because of how strong the feature is currently \npresenting across the SERN states. I think of it like trying to \nshove a beach-ball under water. Sure, it will compress, but it \nstill wants to be big and spreads out. So, maybe we see some \nsubtropical influence the second half of next week into next \nweekend, but it is possible that the ridge cuts off the moisture\nsource and we remain dry. Plenty of uncertainty, so I think the\nbest course of action for Wednesday through Friday is to \nmaintain a chance of showers and thunderstorms due to re-cycling\nmoisture.",
